
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Growing up and watching Star Trek, one thing that made it feel futuristic to me was the interaction with the &#8220;computer.&#8221; You didn&#8217;t see Captain Picard updating software or managing user interfaces. Instead, he just said something out into the air, and then the computer responded politely and efficiently, giving him whatever he needed.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">At the time, that made total sense to me as something that would be natural in a few hundred years, but it never occurred to me that we would get there in my lifetime. And yet, we are rapidly approaching the Star Trek moment.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Through a variety of technologies – flawless text-to-speech dictation, modern processors, and artificial intelligence – we are getting to that point where you can just yell something into the air and digital problems get solved. Heck, if you&#8217;re willing to go out on the end of the spear, we are already there.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In essence, this lets you separate the user from the software. It&#8217;s putting another level of abstraction between you and the work. This doesn&#8217;t mean we&#8217;re replacing the software,&nbsp;<em>at least for the time being</em>, but it does mean you can step away from it. Rather than being an&nbsp;<em>operator</em>&nbsp;of your software and systems, you become more of a&nbsp;<em>director</em>&nbsp;of it.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Smart productivity app developers are already seeing that, and that&#8217;s why you&#8217;re seeing so many of them release MCPs and other techniques for their software to work with artificial intelligence. They are, in effect, adding their software to your voice-controlled stack.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I expect that to only accelerate. We&#8217;ll be getting Siri AI very shortly, and you&#8217;ll see how Apple is doing the same thing within the confines of their own operating system and, of course, under their terms.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">We are spending a lot of time talking about these technologies in the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.macsparky.com/join/">MacSparky Labs</a>&nbsp;and the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.macsparky.com/rafg/">Robot Assistant Field Guide</a>. Over time, what this will achieve is to make all of us power users provided we can get comfortable with the additional abstraction and use our imaginations. What I&#8217;m here to tell you is that normal folks in the not-so-distant future are going to work very similarly to Jean-Luc Picard.</p>



